Propionibacterium acnes (now Cutibacterium acnes) is a slow-growing, anaerobic gram-positive rod. It is a known cause of chronic, low-grade postoperative endophthalmitis, often associated with intraocular lenses (IOLs). The clinical picture of recurrent inflammation, particularly with a hypopyon, after cataract surgery (implied by YAG capsulotomy for posterior capsule opacification) is highly suggestive of chronic bacterial endophthalmitis. The positive culture for P. acnes confirms this diagnosis.

P. acnes endophthalmitis is notoriously difficult to treat because the organism is slow-growing and can reside within the capsular bag, often protected from intravitreal antibiotics. Standard treatment involves long-term intravitreal antibiotics (typically vancomycin or clindamycin) and often systemic antibiotics. However, the source of infection, the IOL and capsular bag, often needs to be removed for definitive cure, especially in cases of recurrent or persistent infection despite appropriate antibiotic therapy.

