The patient is a 63-year-old man with a significant smoking history and alcohol dependence presenting with left eye pain and redness. The eye examination reveals conjunctival and scleral hyperemia, dilated scleral vessels, and scleral thinning with an epithelial defect. Systemic symptoms include weight loss, night sweats, and cough. Laboratory tests show positive proteinase 3–antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody (PR3-ANCA) and positive interferon γ release assay (IGRA) using QuantiFERON-TB gold. Chest CT shows small pulmonary nodules. Sputum cultures and PCR for Mycobacterium tuberculosis are negative.

The eye findings (scleritis, specifically necrotizing scleritis given the thinning and epithelial defect) in the context of systemic symptoms (weight loss, night sweats, cough) and positive PR3-ANCA strongly suggest granulomatosis with polyangiitis (GPA), formerly known as Wegener's granulomatosis. GPA is a systemic vasculitis that commonly affects the upper respiratory tract, lungs, and eyes. Scleritis is a common ocular manifestation of GPA. The positive PR3-ANCA is highly specific for GPA.

